text:
anika: Heads-up — customer reports duplicate charges when using the TS SDK from a long-lived worker. They see a 401, the SDK refreshes token, and then retries which sometimes result in duplicate POSTs.

mike: Any repro code from them? that pattern smells like a shared AbortController or signal being reused across requests.

customer-jess: Pasting a minimal repro (we run this in a GCP Cloud Run instance):
```ts
// simplified repro - DO NOT USE this pattern
import fetch from 'node-fetch';
let sharedController = new AbortController();
let token = 'initial-token';
setInterval(async () => {
  // rotate token every 30s in our app
  token = await getFreshToken();
}, 30000);

async function callApi() {
  // reusing the same signal across calls
  const res = await fetch('https://api.redwood.example/v1/generate', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
    body: JSON.stringify({ prompt: 'hi' }),
    signal: sharedController.signal,
  });
  return res.json();
}

// hammer it
setInterval(() => callApi().catch(e => console.log('err', e.message)), 500);
```
logs:
```
2026-01-15T10:00:00Z request:start id=1
2026-01-15T10:00:00Z response:401 id=1
2026-01-15T10:00:00Z auth:refresh start
2026-01-15T10:00:00Z sharedController.abort() // our app sometimes aborts on refresh
2026-01-15T10:00:00Z retry:send id=1-retry
2026-01-15T10:00:00Z response:200 id=1-retry
2026-01-15T10:00:00Z charge:created id=1-retry
```

customer-jess: So sometimes the original partial request still completes on the server and we end up with two charges.

simon-eng-oncall: Thanks, that log is super helpful. Two likely issues: (1) app-level aborts cancel the shared signal and cause the SDK to retry, but the server may have already accepted the original request before the abort propagated, and (2) token refresh mutation + reuse of the same controller/signal across in-flight requests.

mike: We should confirm how our TS SDK treats incoming signals. Does it call signal.abort() internally anywhere? Also check that we don't attach the same controller to both the original request and a retry.

dev-priyanka: Looking at the SDK code: we accept an optional { signal } from callers. If missing, we create a new AbortController per attempt. But there's a bug in our retry wrapper: when an auth-refresh path triggers a retry we call `signal?.abort()` to cancel the previous attempt, but if the caller passed a shared signal we end up aborting the caller's controller too. That surface is dangerous.

anika: So root cause: SDK aborting a caller-supplied signal during internal retry flow + consumers reusing a single controller across requests. That combo leads to canceled local state and racing replays that the server may have processed.

mike: Repro plan for us: 1) run the customer's snippet locally, 2) instrument request ids and server acceptance ordering to confirm duplicate accept. I can reproduce locally in ~30s with the shared controller.

dev-priyanka: Quick mitigation: update the SDK to never call abort() on a caller-provided signal. Instead, create an internal controller and, if a caller signal is provided, wire it so that caller abort cancels both but internal aborts only cancel internal controller. Also add idempotency-key support for POSTs and ensure retry logic surfaces when we retry after auth refresh.

customer-jess: Appreciate the quick explanation. Is there a recommended app-side change we can do immediately?

mike: Short-term: don't reuse an AbortController across multiple requests. Create a fresh AbortController per request (or omit passing a signal unless you really need to cancel). Also add an idempotency-key header on write endpoints if your backend supports it.

dev-priyanka: Patch plan: PR to change abort behavior + add a warning in changelog and a runtime dev warning when a shared signal is detected. We'll also ship a minor release that documents idempotency as a best practice.

deploy-bot: release/ts-sdk v0.14.2 created by dev-priyanka — tests passed — queued for canary deploy.

anika: Labeling this as customer-escalation, assigning follow-up to dev-priyanka to confirm customer after canary.

customer-jess: Thanks — we'll switch to per-request controller and add idempotency keys while you roll the patch.

simon-eng-oncall: Follow-up note for records: root cause summary: SDK should never abort user-supplied signals. Combined with consumer code that reused a single AbortController across calls + auth refresh/retry path, that led to canceled client-side flows and duplicate server-side processing. Remediation: SDK change + app guidance + encourage idempotency on POSTs.

anika: Closing loop once customer confirms. :white_check_mark:
